00:00:17.900 --> 00:00:20.570 align:start position:0%
 
welcome to the Macmillan report I'm  Marilyn Wilkes your host and our guest  is Alan McHale an assistant professor of  history at Yale University he is a  historian of the early modern Muslim  world the Ottoman Empire and Egypt whose  research and teaching focused mostly on  the nature of early modern imperial rule  peasant histories environmental resource  management and science and medicine in  one of the first environmental histories  of the Ottoman Empire  professor McHale examines relations  between the Empire and its most  lucrative province of Egypt today we  talk with him about his award-winning  new book nature and empire in Ottoman  Egypt an environmental history  welcome professor McHale thanks very  much this is your first book and I think  it's such a novel idea what led you to  write it at the most basic level I was  interested in the history of the Ottoman  Empire in the Arab world the Ottoman  Empire ruled in most parts of the Arab  world for about 350 years so I was  curious about the obvious impact that  must have had on the region and then  also the region's impact on the Ottoman  Empire as a whole and on imperial rule  in in different parts of the Empire okay  and where does the Ottoman Empire fall  in terms of history what years and then  what was going on in Egypt during that  time okay so the conventional dates that  are given for the Empire are 1300 to  1922 okay so that's over 600 years and  the Ottoman Empire existed in what we  today called the Middle East in the  Balkans and in parts of North Africa  okay so it was a late medieval state an  early modern state a 19th century  modernizing state and then a participant  in World War one so it has a huge  a huge history in multiple places very  different sorts of regions with  different languages different cultural  traditions different religions so it  touched on many different parts of you  know what we now call the old world so  Europe and the Balkans obviously Africa  Iran and Central Asia Russia the  Mediterranean the Indian Ocean etc okay  and give us an overview of your book  okay a little bit about it okay so the  book as you mentioned in the  introduction is interested in at the  most general level of the relationship  between Egypt and the rest of the  Ottoman Empire to get at that  relationship I wrote an environmental  history because I came to see that that  was the best way of getting at this  relationship so Egypt was conquered by  Ottoman armies in 1517 okay  made one of the most important provinces  of the Empire over its history it proved  to be the most lucrative province in the  Ottoman Empire and then remained within  the ottoman fold historians disagree  about this but until the end of the 19th  century some said the beginning of the  19th century but I say the end of the of  the 19th century so my work is really  focused on the 17th and the 18th  centuries and I examine in each chapter  a different aspect of natural resource  management so this included water  obviously in Egypt the Nile is very  important so irrigation this also  included the production of food  resources so grains mostly and then also  I'm interested in the way that the  Empire managed the movement of wood to  the province of Egypt because Egypt was  an excessive producer of grain but  didn't have a lot of native timber  supplies which were needed for various  irrigation works other building projects  other sort of infrastructural  infrastructural projects so irrigation  tied Egypt in a very intimate way to the  rest of the Empire so we see a great  interest on  part of the central administration of  the capital in Istanbul modern-day  Turkey in the sort of nitty-gritty  day-to-day on-the-ground management of  even the smallest canals in very rural  parts of Egypt so we have a very strong  bureaucratic connection over the  management of water and the reason for  that was because Egypt was such such an  important producer of grain for feeding  different parts of the Empire so first I  examine the management of water and then  I show how food was moved to different  parts of the Empire how wood was brought  from from different parts of the Empire  to Egypt so the opposite direction  mostly from Anatolia southern Anatolia  so what is Turkey today and then also  from what what today is Lebanon and  parts of Syria and then I'm also  interested in in the labor that  undertook all of this and how that is  managed and then I end the book with an  examination of how this system changed  from the 18th century to the early 19th  century and how the management of  natural resources moved from being  controlled by local communities within  Egypt within an Imperial framework but  nevertheless on the ground by local  local communities to being managed more  centrally from Cairo and the  implications of that both for politics  for economic relations within the Empire  and then also for environmental  management okay and it's tell us about  your methodology it seems like it it  would be difficult to find all of these  types of information how did you go  about that  so before I began this project I was  also a little worried that these sorts  of issues would not would not be  adequately addressed by the archival  materials that we have on this period  because many historians obviously have  worked on this period none have really  dealt with environmental history or  environmental resource management now  looking back on it I see that you know  my fears were a little misplaced because  obviously irrigation had such a  Imperial such white imperial importance  and so there are many many records of it  so for my research I used archival  collections both in Istanbul so the  central repository of the Ottoman state  is in is in Istanbul and then I also use  the the records of local Islamic courts  throughout Egypt so I wanted to operate  both at an imperial level and then also  a local level and bring the two together  so that required using archival  collections in Ottoman Turkish and in  Arabic in Istanbul and then also in  Egypt when you looked at the records the  individual records what you know  basically what is it showing you mm-hmm  okay so for example in the core records  okay  you would have peasants from a certain  community coming and saying we are the  we're the farmers of village X and the  upstream village village Y has been  taking more water from the canal than is  their their historic allotted amount  therefore less water is coming to us so  we're not able to irrigate our fields in  the way that we need to to produce the  food that ultimately you the Ottoman  state will tax so you need to help us to  deal with this situation Wow very  interesting that they have you know such  detailed records like that can you give  us an example of how environmental  history helps us understand the Ottoman  Empire okay  usually Ottoman historians are  interested in a certain part of the  Ottoman Empire a province and in doing  that provinces history and yes in  relation to the Ottoman Empire but  really a history of that province where  they're interested in doing a history of  the capital in its institutions so I was  really attracted to doing an  environmental history of the Ottoman  Empire because it allows us to do I  think in a sort of integrative history  so for example I examine cases of the  building of dams in Egypt again to help  irrigate fields that would grow food  that would be shipped  to different parts of the Empire to  North Africa to parts of the Balkans to  parts of Anatolia etc mm-hmm  but because Egypt doesn't have native  wood supplies that would have to be  brought from elsewhere so I traced a  particular case in the 1720s of the  importation of wood from parts of  southwestern Anatolia so around what is  today Izmir and that would being brought  to Egypt then being shipped down the  Nile and then taken to a certain a  certain village to build a small dam so  in tracing this history it allows us to  connect the history of the harvesting of  wood and for instance the labor of the  Lumberjacks  in in south western Anatolia who  harvested the wood the the merchant  ships that brought that wood across the  Mediterranean to Egypt the sailors who  sailed it down the Nile ultimately to  the people who used it in the  countryside in Egypt to build this dam  that again it will irrigate water to  grow food that will be sent to different  parts of the Empire so environmental  history allows us to operate at these  different scales of the village right  the small scale nitty-gritty community  of the village all the way up to the  Mediterranean as a unit of analysis and  different parts of the Empire and it  allows us to bring in different actors  so in this case the Lumberjacks in  Anatolia and the farmers in in Egypt  very good and conclusions what are some  of the conclusions you reach in your  book so probably the main conclusion  that I reach is that the the local  management of these natural resource  processes was ultimately from from the  perspective of those involved more  equitable more sustainable and  ultimately better for their lives and  what do I mean by that in the 19th  century when much of the management of  irrigation was centralized by the  Imperial bureaucracy in Cairo this led  to the large-scale movement  of peasants brought to work on canal  projects rather than in the previous  centuries these canals being managed  locally it led to the salinization of  soil on these projects and ultimately it  led to less autonomy for these local  communities so I think there's a lesson  that can be exported to places outside  of the Ottoman Empire and outside of  Egypt in terms of the the local  oversight over not just natural  resources but over let's say local  resources more generally and what  happens when management of local  resources becomes more centralized or  more bureaucratized by some kind of  central administration yeah that makes  sense  final question I always like to know in  doing the research for the book was  there anything that particularly  surprised you I think the thing that  probably surprised me most was the level  of detail that I was able to find in  these different sources again we find  the enumeration of hundreds and hundreds  of people you know to the single  individual we find the weighing of for  instance rice down to the very minor  scale and there's just copious copious  amounts of sources about very small  scale nitty gritty kinds of things and  it's often said that the Ottoman Empire  sailed on a sea of paper and and that's  true we're just beginning to scratch the  surface of the sources that we have for  Ottoman history so I knew going in that  I would find a lot but the the level of  detail I think is what what really  surprised me so are you planning a step  number two after this book stand some of  the things that you found I am so in  reading these cases of irrigation repair  projects for the most part  I came to see that animal labor was used  in lots of these projects so used to  bring materials to the work site used to  Holloway dirt used sometimes even in the  dredging of canals and I found that  curious at first now again it's not it's  not very surprising when I say it and  then I started sort of cluing into  animals and I noticed that they were all  over the sources that I was reading so  for instance in the Egyptian countryside  the ownership of animals represented the  most significant form of capital for  rural Egyptians because technically the  ownership of technically they did not  own the land that they used but animals  represented some of their most important  forms of property so in my next book I  want to trace the importance of animals  to this rural economy so I think we  can't understand an early modern  agrarian society without understanding  the really important and multiple roles  that animals played in the society and  then I think there are important changes  that happen in in the latter part of the  18th century so I want to I want to work  on that and trace that in my next book  very good well we'll look forward to  that thank you so much for being here  with us today and sharing some of your  work for more information about  professor Mikhail and his research  please visit our website at Yale edu  backslash Macmillan report be sure to  join us again for another episode of the  Macmillan report made possible through  funding from the Whitney and Betty  Macmillan Centre for International and  area studies at Yale

